# Scope

A parallel mediation model
will be used as an example to
illustrate how to use `semeqmodels`.

# Original Model

Suppose this is the original model we fitted
to the dataset `data_test_4_factor_3_item`
(installed with the package):

```{r demo_lav_4_model, echo = FALSE, fig.cap = "Parallel Mediation Model"}
library(semeqmodels)
library(semPlot)
library(semptools)
suppressMessages(library(lavaan))
mod <-
"
fm1 ~ fx
fm2 ~ fx
fy ~ fm1 + fm2 + fx
fx =~ x1 + x2 + x3
fm1 =~ m1 + m2 + m3
fm2 =~ m4 + m5 + m6
fy =~ y1 + y2 + y3
# fm1 ~~ fm2
"
fit0 <- sem(mod, data_test_4_factor_3_item, do.fit = FALSE)
layout_m <- matrix(c(NA, "fm1", NA,
                     "fx", NA, "fy",
                     NA, "fm2", NA), 3, 3, byrow = TRUE)
p <- semPaths(fit0,
              layout = layout_m,
              nCharNodes = 0,
              exoCov = FALSE,
              residuals = FALSE,
              sizeLat = 10,
              asize = 5,
              structural = TRUE,
              DoNotPlot = TRUE)
p2 <- p |>
 set_curve(c("fm1 ~~ fm2" = -2))
plot(p2)
text(
  x = 0,
  y = -1.25,
  "(Only latent variables are shown.)"
)
```

This is a model:

```{r}
mod <-
"
fx =~ x1 + x2 + x3
fm1 =~ m1 + m2 + m3
fm2 =~ m4 + m5 + m6
fy =~ y1 + y2 + y3
fm1 ~ fx
fm2 ~ fx
fy ~ fm1 + fm2 + fx
"
```

This is the lavaan results for the model:

```{r}
library(lavaan)
fit <- sem(
  model = mod,
  data = data_test_4_factor_3_item
)
fit
```

```{r load_empeq, child=c("demo_ins_empeq.txt")}
```

```{r load_genmodels, child=c("demo_ins_genmodels.txt")}
```

```{r load_drawmodels, child=c("demo_ins_drawmodels.txt")}
```

```{r}
# Set the layout of the plots
m <- matrix(
  c(  NA, "fm1",   NA,
    "fx",   NA, "fy",
      NA, "fm2",   NA),
  nrow = 3,
  ncol = 3,
  byrow = TRUE)
m
```

We can then generate the plots:

```{r}
p <- partables_plots(
  out,
  original_model = fit,
  structural = TRUE,
  layout = m,
  label.cex = 1.2,
  sizeLat = 11,
  edge.width = 5,
  asize = 5,
  curve_cov_settings = list(base_curve = 4)
)
```

The argument `curve_cov_settings`,
with `list(base_curve = 4)`, is used to
make the curves for covariances easier
to read.

```{r load_plotmodels, child=c("demo_ins_plotmodels.txt")}
```


```{r demo_lav_4_eqmodels, fig.width = 8, fig.height = 6, echo = TRUE, fig.cap = "Empirically Equivalent Models"}
plot(
  p,
  ncol = 4,
  nrow = 3,
  title_adj = 2
)
```

